  "worked_solution": "We wish to subtract the following fractions:\n\n$$\na-b=\\frac{2}{3p}-\\frac{3x}{p}\n$$\n\nIn order to subtract the two fractions, we need them to have a common denominator. Here, we can put both fractions over a common denominator of $3p$ if we multiply the top and bottom of the second fraction by $3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3p}-\\frac{3x}{p} & = \\\\ \\frac{2}{3p}-\\frac{3}{3}\\cdot \\frac{3x}{p} & = \\\\ \\frac{2}{3p}-\\frac{9x}{3p} & = \\\\ \\frac{2-9x}{3p} & \\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
